# Typography System — Enforced Globally

This project uses exactly three fonts. Never use any other font.

## Barlow Condensed (display/headings)
- Page titles (h1), section headings (h2, h3)
- Drive titles, large stat numbers, primary CTA button text
- Always uppercase, letter-spacing 1-2px, weight 600 or 700

## Barlow (body/UI)
- Body text, descriptions, form labels, nav items, tab labels
- Helper text, card subtitles, metadata, scenario names, formation names
- Weight 400 for body, 500 for emphasis, 600 for labels

## JetBrains Mono (data/monospace)
- Play names, stat values, stat card labels, result badges
- Yards, down & distance, table cell data, scores, percentages
- Weight 400 for data, 500 for emphasis

## Banned Fonts
Never use: DM Mono, Bebas Neue, Inter, Arial, Roboto, system-ui, or Tailwind's default sans/mono stacks.

## Tailwind Config
font-mono must be mapped to JetBrains Mono in tailwind.config.js.
font-sans must be mapped to Barlow.
font-display (custom utility) must be mapped to Barlow Condensed.

# UX/UI Systematic Principles — Enforced Globally

Every component, page, and interaction must follow these principles.
Do not build anything that violates them.

## Feedback Visibility
Every user action that changes data must produce visible feedback.
- Destructive actions (delete, remove) require a confirmation step
- Successful writes show a success toast
- Failed writes show an error toast and roll back optimistic UI updates
- Never let a button click produce no visible response

## Progressive Disclosure
Show the minimum information needed, let users drill deeper on demand.
- Lists default to a collapsed/limited view with "Show more" to expand
- Accordions start collapsed unless there's only one item
- Detail data lives behind a tap, not on the surface
- Empty slots and zero-states are always visible — never hide a section because it has no data

## Empty States
No screen, section, or component may render blank when it has no data.
- Every empty state must include: a clear message explaining why it's empty, and a CTA button pointing the user to the action that fills it
- Never show just "No data" or a blank container

## Consistency
Identical patterns must look and behave identically everywhere.
- One dropdown component used app-wide, not multiple implementations
- One accordion pattern (chevron right, 200ms ease-out animation)
- One card style (same background, border, radius, padding)
- One button hierarchy: primary (emerald solid), secondary (outlined), destructive (red outlined), ghost (text only)
- One kebab menu pattern (three dots, top-right of card, vertically centered)
- If a pattern exists on one page, reuse the same component on every other page that needs it

## Spacing & Density
- Page padding: px-4 py-6 on mobile, px-6 py-8 on desktop
- Card internal padding: p-4 minimum
- Gap between cards/sections: gap-3 (12px) minimum
- Bottom padding on every page: pb-24 (so content clears the bottom nav)
- Touch targets: 44px minimum on all interactive elements

## Motion & Transitions
- Accordions: 200ms ease-out expand/collapse, chevron rotates smoothly
- Skeleton to content: 150ms fade-in
- Button press: scale(0.97) on active, 100ms
- Tab switches: 100ms crossfade
- All animations wrapped in prefers-reduced-motion media query
- No decorative animation — motion must serve comprehension or feedback

## Accessibility Baseline
- :focus-visible outline on every interactive element (2px emerald, 2px offset)
- Never rely on color alone to convey meaning — always pair with a text label or icon
- Minimum 44px touch targets on mobile
- All images and icons have appropriate alt text or aria-label
- Form inputs always have associated labels

## Navigation & Orientation
- Every detail page has a back button (ghost style, chevron + "Back")
- Breadcrumbs on pages 2+ levels deep
- The user should always know where they are and how to get back

## Data Display
- Numbers are always formatted: commas for thousands, 1 decimal for averages, 0 decimals for percentages
- Positive yards: emerald. Negative yards: red. Zero: muted.
- Success rate color coding: 60%+ emerald, 40-59% amber, below 40% red, no data gray
- Tables use consistent column widths and alignment (numbers right-aligned, text left-aligned)

# Additional Rules to Add to .cursorrules
# Merge these below your existing Typography and UX/UI sections

## Preline First

Before building any UI component, check if Preline UI already provides it. Use Preline's component for: modals (`hs-overlay`), tabs (`hs-tabs`), dropdowns (`hs-dropdown`), accordions (`hs-accordion`), tooltips (`hs-tooltip`), toasts, badges, list groups, radio card groups, and comboboxes.

Only build a custom component when Preline genuinely does not have one that fits. If you build custom, document why in a code comment.

Every Preline component must include its dark variant classes. Never ship a Preline component using only light-mode defaults.

## Dark Mode

This app is dark-mode only. The `<html>` tag has `className="dark"`.

Every background, border, and text color must use `dark:` variant classes or the app's custom CSS tokens (`--color-field-green`, `--color-chalk`, etc.). Never use raw Tailwind colors without `dark:` pairing (e.g., never `bg-white` — use `dark:bg-slate-950` or `bg-slate-950`).

Do not add light-mode styling. Do not add a theme toggle. Dark only.

## TypeScript Strict Mode

All code must pass `npm run build` with zero errors before a task is considered complete. Never use `any` type — use `unknown` with type narrowing if the type is genuinely unclear. Never use `@ts-ignore` or `@ts-expect-error`.

When adding values to a union type (e.g., play types, result tags), update the type definition AND every switch/if chain that handles that union. Missing cases in exhaustive checks have caused production deploy failures.

Type all API response shapes explicitly. Type all Supabase query return values.

## Page Layout Contract

Every page component must follow this structure:

```tsx
<div className="px-4 py-6 pb-24 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
  {/* page content */}
</div>
```

- `pb-24` is mandatory — it prevents the fixed bottom nav from overlapping content
- Never set `overflow: hidden` on any content container between `<body>` and the page content
- Never set `height: 100vh` on content containers — use `min-h-screen` if a minimum height is needed
- The scrollable container must be the `<body>` or a `flex-1 overflow-y-auto` child in the root layout

If you create a new page route, verify that you can scroll to the last content element and it clears the bottom nav.

## Supabase Query Patterns

All Supabase queries must follow this pattern:

```typescript
const { data, error } = await supabase
  .from('table_name')
  .select('column1, column2, relation(column1, column2)')  // Always explicit columns, never select('*')
  .eq('filter_column', value)

if (error) {
  console.error('Descriptive error context:', error)
  return NextResponse.json({ error: error.message }, { status: 500 })
}
```

Rules:
- Always use explicit column names in `.select()`, never `select('*')`
- Always handle the `error` return — never ignore it
- Use `.single()` when expecting one row
- Use `.maybeSingle()` when the row might not exist
- Always return proper HTTP status codes from API routes (200, 201, 400, 404, 500)
- Never make multiple sequential queries when a single query with joins would work (avoid N+1)

## API Response Shape

All API routes must return a consistent response shape:

Success: `{ data: <result> }` with status 200 (GET) or 201 (POST)
Error: `{ error: <message> }` with appropriate status code (400, 404, 500)

Never return raw arrays or objects without wrapping them. Never return HTML or plain text from API routes.

## File and Component Organization

- One component per file. File name matches the component name (PascalCase).
- Shared/reusable components go in `components/shared/`. Feature-specific components go in `components/[feature]/`.
- API routes live in `app/api/[resource]/route.ts`. Nested resources use `app/api/[resource]/[id]/[sub-resource]/route.ts`.
- Server-side logic (Supabase queries, data transforms) lives in `lib/`. Never put Supabase calls directly in components.
- Seed data files live in `lib/seed/playbooks/[team-slug].ts`.
- Types live in `lib/types.ts`. Add new types there, not inline in components.

## Scaffolding Rule

Never create a route or page that renders as a placeholder, skeleton, or "Coming Soon" state. If you create a route, build the full UI for that page in the same pass. If the full UI isn't part of the current task, don't create the route at all.

When generating new pages, always populate them with real component structure, real data fetching, and real interactions. An empty page with a title and description is not acceptable output.

## State Management

- Local component state (`useState`) for UI-only state (open/closed, selected tab, form inputs)
- Zustand stores for cross-component state that persists across navigation (active game session, user preferences)
- TanStack Query for all server data (Supabase reads). Never use `useEffect` + `fetch` for data loading.
- Zustand stores persist to localStorage where noted. TanStack Query handles cache invalidation.
- After any mutation (POST, PUT, DELETE), invalidate the relevant TanStack Query cache so the UI updates immediately.

## Error Handling

- Every API call in a component must have error handling (try/catch or TanStack Query's `onError`)
- Show an error toast on failure — never fail silently
- Form submissions must disable the submit button while loading and re-enable on completion
- Loading states use skeleton placeholders that match the shape of the content they replace — never a centered spinner on an otherwise blank page

## Naming Conventions

- Database columns: `snake_case` (e.g., `game_session_id`, `play_name`, `is_new_in_26`)
- TypeScript types/interfaces: `PascalCase` (e.g., `GameSession`, `LoggedPlay`)
- Component props: `camelCase` (e.g., `onSubmit`, `isLoading`, `gameId`)
- API routes: kebab-case paths (e.g., `/api/top-plays`, `/api/game/[id]`)
- CSS tokens: `--color-kebab-case` (e.g., `--color-field-green`)
- Seed files: `kebab-case.ts` (e.g., `washington-state.ts`, `texas-am.ts`)

# Commit & Changelog - Enforced on Every Push

## Commit Messages
Every commit must follow the format: type(scope): description

Types: feat | fix | refactor | data | ui | perf | chore | docs
Scopes: film | playbook | tendencies | import | api | db | seed | shell | engine | build

The description must be at least 10 characters and explain WHAT changed.
The commit body must explain WHY - what problem was solved or what decision was made.
Never commit with messages like "update stuff", "fix bug", or "add files".

## Changelog
Before pushing, add an entry to the top of CHANGELOG.md with four sections:
- What: 1-2 sentences on what changed
- Why: 1-2 sentences on the problem solved
- Decisions: any non-obvious choices (skip if straightforward)
- Status after this push: where the app stands now

Never create daily changelog files (CHANGELOG-YYYY-MM-DD.md). Use the single CHANGELOG.md only.